          "explanation": "Rand's politics rests heavily on property rights, yet Cortlandt legally belonged to the government, not Roark. That makes the property objection an internal critique: it asks whether the novel is consistent with itself. Defenders answer that the design was Roark's property and was taken by fraud. The other options are either trivial or miss the moral issue entirely.",
